>
  <p>"Study smart, not hard" is often sold as a shortcut. It is not. It is the same hours spent on methods that work instead of methods that merely feel like work. The hard truth inside the slogan is that the comfortable techniques are usually the useless ones.</p>

  <h2 id="myth">The effort illusion</h2>
  <p>Bjork and colleagues describe how our judgements of learning mislead us. Re-reading makes text feel fluent and familiar, which we mistake for knowing it. Then the exam asks us to produce the answer from a blank page and the familiarity is no help. Smart studying means trusting the evidence over the feeling, even when the better method feels harder and slower.</p>

  <h2 id="habits">7 high-leverage habits</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Test yourself</strong> from memory before checking. This is the big one.</li>
    <li><strong>Space</strong> your sessions across days instead of cramming.</li>
    <li><strong>Interleave</strong> related topics rather than blocking one.</li>
    <li><strong>Use past papers</strong> under timed conditions.</li>
    <li><strong>Explain it simply</strong> to find gaps (the <a href="/guides/feynman-technique/">Feynman technique</a>).</li>
    <li><strong>Review mistakes</strong>, since errors mark your real syllabus.</li>
    <li><strong>Sleep on it</strong>, letting memory consolidate overnight.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="drop">What to stop doing</h2>
  <p>Dunlosky's review rated re-reading, highlighting and summarising as low utility for most students. They are not forbidden, but they should support testing, not replace it. If your revision is mostly colouring a textbook, you are working hard and learning little.</p>

  <h2 id="apply">Put it together</h2>
  <p>Plan sessions across several days with the <a href="/tools/study-plan-generator/">study plan generator</a>, run each as a focused <a href="/tools/pomodoro-timer/">Pomodoro</a> that ends in a self-test, and line the last reviews up with your <a href="/tools/exam-countdown/">exam countdown</a>. For the full research treatment, read <a href="/guides/evidence-based-study-techniques/">active recall and spaced repetition</a>.</p>
</Guide>
